{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "61",
    "case_summary": "A 65-year-old man presented to our hospital in October 2006 with a large abdominal mass. Physical examination revealed a firm mass extending from the epigastrium to the left hypogastrium. Laboratory tests showed a slight elevation in total bilirubin (1.3 mg/dl) and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH: 217 IU/L) levels, while tumor markers carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) and carbohydrate antigen (CA19-9) were within normal limits. Ultrasonography indicated that the mass occupied nearly the entire upper abdomen, positioned anterior to the bowel loops. Computed tomography (CT) revealed a mass behind the left hepatic lobe with heterogeneous low density and faint enhancement. Abdominal angiography demonstrated that the tumor received its blood supply primarily from the right epigastric artery. During laparotomy in October 2006, a well-encapsulated tumor was identified in the greater omentum, with no adhesion to adjacent organs except for a pinpoint adhesion to the stomach. The right gastroepiploic artery and vein were prominent and stretched by the tumor, with a major supply vessel branching from it in one stalk. No metastasis was observed in the abdominal cavity. The tumor was a well-demarcated, reddish-gray solid mass measuring 20 × 17 × 6 cm, with irregular nodularity. The cut surfaces were tan-colored, featuring focal necrotic areas and a cystic nodule.\n\nThe tumor was composed of proliferating epithelioid cells and myxoid cells with an interlacing bundle pattern. The cellularity was relatively high and the frequency of mitotic figures was 2 of 50 high power fields (HPF). The MIB-1 index was 4.4%. The tumor cells were diffusely immunoreactive for myeloid stem cell antigen (CD34), weakly or focally positive for c-kit proto-oncogene protein product (CD117) and slightly positive for neuron-specific enolase (NSE). There was no staining for cytokeratin (CK), alpha-smooth muscle actin (SMA) or S-100 protein. Mutations in the platelet-derived growth factor alpha (PDGFRA) gene exon 12, codon 561, encoding a thymine to adenine substitution.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
